  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Brazil</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">In Brazil, our work will encompass national and subnational influencing to diminish and restrict agribusiness exploitative practices and to increase support for agroecology. We will also use our influence to push for more guarantees and protections for land rights, including FPIC, and more support for land titling and land reform. Another focus of our influencing will be o regulating businessand human rights, from human rights due diligence, to deforestation, environmental licensing and other areas of regulating businessactivities to diminish their impacts on human rights, food, land and climate. We will carry out public and private sector influencing based on evidence and action-oriented research. We will also develop consistent public engagement actions, including at least twocampaigning moments (potentially in year 2 and year 3. These campaigning moments will potentially focus on pesticides and agroecology and on climate transition with zero evictions, including asks for governments and for privatesector players. Overall, our public engagement will seek to generate more support for agroecology, to consolidate a constituency of supporters that fight for a just andsustainable food system and to generate accountability for private sector and public sector players for their unstainable policies,practices and support for false climate solutions. Internationally, we will engage in key international for a on the issues of human rights and environmental due diligence, climate, development, land and agriculture. We will try to use our space with the current Brazilian government, which is aligned with a rights-based agenda, to leverage our positions. Key influencing moments will be the high-level international forums happening in Brazil G20 in 2024, Climate COP in 2025 and the Brics+ summit in 2026. OBR&#x2019;s influencing work will be grounded on community level work done together with our key partner Comiss&#xe3;o Pastoral da Terra, CPT NE II (land pastoral community northeast II). CPT NE II is the CPT chapter forfive Brazilian states in the Northeast. During the project we will also explore other ad hoc partnerships considering the context and the influencing needs. We anticipate as potential partners MPA (movement of smallholder farmers), Joio e Trigo (investigative journalism independent outlet), UFRRJ CPDA (university research center on agricultural policy issues, including agroecology), feminist organizations dedicated to agroecology like SOF and Centro 8 de Marco etc.</narrative>
  </description>
  <participating-org role="4" type="90">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Oxfam Brasil</narrative>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Oxfam International is a confederationorganisations working together in more than 94 countries to find lasting solutions to poverty, suffering and injustice. Oxfam Brazil is one of them. With many of the causes of poverty global in nature, members of Oxfam International believe they can achieve greater impact in addressing issues of poverty by their collective efforts.To achieve the maximum impact on poverty, the Oxfams link up their work on development programs, humanitarian response, lobbying for policy changes at national and global level. The Oxfams popular campaigns and communications work is aimed at mobilising public opinion for change.
Oxfam Brazil is a global and Brazilian civil society organization that works together with and mobilizes people, groups, organizations and institutional bodies. It makes political alliances, encourages the convergence of actors, provides financial support, conducts research, produces, disseminates and brokers knowledge, informing public opinion and mobilizing citizens and organizations to leverage change. It wants to change the conditions that generate and perpetuate inequality and poverty, while respecting the sustainability of the planet.</narrative>
  </participating-org>
